Subject: Size a base class as the type it is written as

`<div rustbindgen replaces="X"></div>` tells bindgen to bind `X` as some other
type the header declares, and that other type is usually a different size: the
annotation exists to substitute a Rust-shaped stand-in for a C++ type Rust
cannot express. A struct holding such a type as a *member* is kept honest.
`FieldData::codegen` measures the field as the type it is written as - resolved
through the `ResolvedTypeRef` the replacement is recorded as, through a typedef
in front of it, through the class template it is an instantiation of - and the
struct is padded out to the size clang reported. A struct *deriving* from one
was not.

A base class is written as a field of the base's own type, and that field goes
through the same substitution: `to_rust_ty_or_opaque` follows the
`ResolvedTypeRef` like any other. The measurement did not follow it.
`StructLayoutTracker::saw_base` was handed the type the class was declared to
derive from, and that type goes on carrying the layout clang gave it, so

    struct Real { unsigned long long a, b, c; };
    /** <div rustbindgen replaces="Real"></div> */
    struct Stand { void* p; };
    struct Derived : Real { unsigned n; };

came out as `struct Derived { _base: Stand, n: c_uint }` - twelve bytes of
content, rounded to sixteen by the pointer's alignment - for a class an LP64
target lays out at thirty-two. The tracker believed the base had filled the
twenty-four bytes the target gave it, so no padding was written in front of `n`
and `pad_struct` had nothing left to add. The class is half the width of the
object C++ builds in it, its own member is at the wrong offset, and every object
crossing the boundary is truncated. A caller who asks C++ to construct one in
storage sized from the Rust type gets it built past the end.

Measure the base the way a field of the same type is measured, through
`field_representation` and `field_representation_layout`. Every route a member
takes to a substitution a base takes as well, and they are the same routes, so
the two now agree by construction rather than by two implementations of the walk
agreeing. Where the field is written as the type the class was declared to
derive from, the walk lands back on it and the measurement is what it was.

The probe deciding whether the bases have to become one opaque field is measured
the same way, for the same reason: what it compares against the offset of the
first member is where the fields written for the bases end, and those fields are
the stand-ins. A class whose base fields are written as the types it was
declared to derive from decides exactly as it did before, and one whose
substitute is *wider* than what it stands in for - which overruns the room the
target gave the bases, and was written out at that width anyway - now reaches
the arm which was written for exactly that overrun.

That leaves the other direction, which no base could take before this: fields
written for the bases which end *before* the bytes the target gave them. The
difference is a run of bytes no field covers, and something has to cover it or
every member after the bases is early. `saw_field` does close such a gap in
front of a member, from the member's own offset, but not always as itself and
not always at all: the blob it asks for is a whole number of units of the
member's alignment, which is longer than the run wherever the two do not divide;
a packed class has that padding suppressed, being a class whose fields are not
aligned; and a bitfield allocation unit is laid out from where the tracker has
reached rather than from an offset of its own, so nothing is written in front of
one. Write the run out where the bases end instead, in bytes, so that it is the
number of bytes it is.

Which classes those are is asked directly, by measuring the bases a second time
as the types the class was declared to derive from. A base written as the type
it was declared as is as wide as the room the target gave it, so the gap it
leaves in front of the first member is the slack that member's own alignment
takes up and is left exactly where it was; the two measurements part company
only where the field is written as something else. A `replaces=` substitute is
the shape this is about, and an alias carrying an `aligned` attribute of its own
is the other: the walk follows the alias to the type it names, whose alignment
is the smaller of the two, and such a class is written with typed bases and this
run of bytes where it used to have its bases collapsed to opaque ones. Which
fields carry the layout differs; the offsets both are built from are the ones
the target reported.

Both ends count a base at its size rounded up to its own alignment, which is the
width of a Rust type of that layout. A layout whose size is not a whole number
of its alignment is not one any type has - only an `aligned` attribute on a
typedef of a narrower type reaches one - and what `helpers::blob` writes for it
agrees above four-byte alignment and is shorter below, where it hands back whole
units of the alignment and drops the remainder. Those classes come out short by
the bytes it dropped, which is fewer than they were short of before. Counting
the size clang measured instead makes the first of the two twice the width C++
gives it, the bytes between the two ends being counted once by the field and
again by the run.

What this does not reach is a substituted base followed by *another* base. The
field written for that second base still starts where the first one's stand-in
ends rather than where the target puts it. The class reaches the size the target
gave it and every member the class declares itself is at the offset it reported,
because the run of bytes above is measured from where the emitted bases end; a
field written for a later base is not. That is the shape the `members_start`
comment below already records as out of reach: catching it takes the offset of
each base, which libclang has reported only since 20.0, through
`clang_getOffsetOfBase`.

